why 1 0 RANGE The bass starts high, leaping up to D♭ and walking down D♭–C–B♭–A♭. Bach often begins a chorale's bass in this lighter, higher register.
why 1 3/4 DOUBLING Alto (B♭–C) and tenor (D♭–C) meet on C by step; the tenor's D♭ was a seventh and had to fall.
why 1 7/8 SPACING The alto stays high on C while the tenor walks down C–B♭–A♭. The gap lasts two eighths and closes as the tenor jumps up to E♭.
why 2 0 SPACING The same gap at its widest; the tenor leaps up to E♭ on the next beat.
why 5 1/2 DOUBLING Soprano (B♭–C) and alto (D♭–C) meet on C by step from opposite sides.
why 7 1/2 SEVENTH The soprano's B♭ is the fifth of the dominant E♭ chord, not a seventh; the tenor's C under it is passing. The tune rises B♭–C–D♭–E♭ as written.
why 7 3/4 SIMILAR_MOTION Everything rises toward B♭ minor (ii): the bass's A♮ is the leading tone of B♭ and the tune climbs to E♭. Each line has its own reason to rise.
why 8 0 LEAPS The bass climbs E–F–G–A♭–A–B♭, a rising line full of leading tones that drives into B♭ minor. The tritone between its ends is not heard as a leap.
why 8 1/2 LEAPS The alto climbs E♭–F–G–A by step to the leading tone of B♭. A scale line hides the tritone between its ends.
why 10 0 UNRESOLVED_NCT The alto strikes G again over the F minor chord and falls to F: a suspension struck anew instead of tied, resolving down by step.
why 11 1/2 SEVENTH As in bar 7, the soprano's B♭ is the fifth of the dominant, not a seventh, and the tune rises to C.
why 11 1/2 DOUBLING Soprano (B♭–C) and alto (D♭–C) meet on C by step from opposite sides.
why 12 1/4 LEAPS After leaping up to F the tenor turns back down by a fifth to B♭ instead of a step: B♭ is the fifth the dominant chord needs.
why 12 1/2 SIMILAR_MOTION The final cadence: every voice falls into the A♭ chord, the tenor's D♭ (a seventh) to C and the bass to a low A♭.
